### Forgot Password Flow Test
### Make sure Redis and MongoDB are running before testing

### 1. Register a test user (if not already exists)
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test@example.com",
  "username": "testuser123",
  "password": "TestPass123!",
  "firstname": "Test",
  "lastname": "User"
}

### 2. Request password reset
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test@example.com"
}

### 3. Verify OTP (replace 123456 with the actual OTP from email)
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test@example.com",
  "otp": "123456"
}

### 4. Reset password with new password
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/reset-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test@example.com",
  "otp": "123456",
  "newPassword": "NewTestPass123!"
}

### 5. Test login with new password
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "testuser123",
  "password": "NewTestPass123!"
}

### Test with non-existent email (should still return success for security)
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nonexistent@example.com"
}

### Test with invalid OTP
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/verify-otp
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test@example.com",
  "otp": "000000"
}

### Test rate limiting (make multiple requests quickly)
POST http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/forgot-password
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "test@example.com"
}
